  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Getting a perfect paint touch-up can feel like a magic trick, but it's really just about knowing the right steps. First off, you've got to clean the area really well. Any dirt or grime will mess with your paint adhesion and how it looks. After that, take care of any small dings or dents before you even think about paint. You want a smooth canvas! Next, and this is crucial, use the exact same type of applicator that was used for the original paint job. If it was rolled, roll your touch-up. If it was brushed, brush it. This helps match the texture so the repair doesn't stand out. Apply a very, very small amount of paint, just enough to cover the blemish. Then, gently feather the edges so it blends into the surrounding wall without a harsh line. Don't go for a thick coat! Remember, paint dries pretty quickly, but it can take up to 24 hours to fully cure, and the sheen can keep developing for a few days. If you find yourself struggling with blending or getting the right finish, that's when a professional touch from HH Remodeling LLC can make all the difference."
    }
  ],
